harbour-seaprint (1.3.4-meego4) unstable; urgency=low

  * The conversion runs in a process of its own. A big scanned PDF peaks at
    34 MB and a page of raster at printer resolution needs far more, while the
    user interface holds some 35 MB and an N950 in daily use has about 70 MB
    free -- so the two together sat at the edge of the device, and when the
    kernel had to take something it took the app in the middle of the job.
    That looked like the app quitting by itself, with nothing in the log.
    Now the app starts itself again with --convert, hands the parameters over
    on stdin and reads the converted document back from stdout: measured on
    the N950, the app's own footprint during a print went from 34 MB down to
    10 MB, with the 29 MB in a child that asks the kernel to take it first
    (oom_adj) and dies with the job. If it is taken, the app is still there
    and says so ("the memory was probably not enough") instead of vanishing.
  * A conversion that gives up no longer hangs the worker thread. The IPP
    request is ended before the failure is reported -- curl was left waiting
    for the rest of a chunked body that was never coming, and the busy page
    stayed up until the app was killed.

 -- Sebastian Matkovich <sebastian.matkovich@gmail.com>  Sat, 17 Oct 2026 00:30:00 +0000

harbour-seaprint (1.3.4-meego3) unstable; urgency=low

  * The printer search asks again. A query and its answer are one UDP packet
    each, and about one in ten went missing over this device's wifi -- with
    four exchanges needed (PTR, TXT, SRV, A) before a printer can be listed,
    a single round of queries left the list empty a good part of the time,
    and nothing ever asked a second time. Whatever has not been answered is
    now asked again after 1, 2, 4 and 8 seconds; a tap on Refresh is no
    longer swallowed by the one-second guard against duplicate queries; and
    a datagram that cannot be parsed no longer leaves every datagram behind
    it unread.
  * A printer whose attributes cannot be read is asked twice more, instead of
    sitting in the list without a name until the page is left and opened
    again. An HP LaserJet 400 answers with an empty body and HTTP 500 now and
    then -- which curl reports as a success and the parser as "Tried to read
    past end" -- while the very next request is answered in full. What could
    not be read is written to the cache directory for
    meego/tests/run-parse-test.sh.
  * The log keeps the run before it as harbour-seaprint.log.1, and stderr
    goes into the log as well: curl's own output, poppler's warnings and
    whatever a dying runtime says on its way out never went through Qt's
    message handler and were simply lost -- and an app that dies is started
    again, which is what used to wipe the only evidence of the death.

 -- Sebastian Matkovich <sebastian.matkovich@gmail.com>  Fri, 16 Oct 2026 17:00:00 +0000

harbour-seaprint (1.3.4-meego2) unstable; urgency=low

  * The launcher icon is now built inside the tree: meego/icons/make-icon.py
    cuts the Sailfish artwork to the exact silhouette of the stock apps,
    taken from a stock icon's alpha channel, drawn at 4x and scaled down.

 -- Sebastian Matkovich <sebastian.matkovich@gmail.com>  Sat, 26 Sep 2026 14:00:00 +0000

harbour-seaprint (1.3.4-meego1) unstable; urgency=low

  * First MeeGo Harmattan edition, from upstream 1.3.4.
  * Qt 4.7 / QtQuick 1.1 user interface with com.nokia.meego components;
    the Silica types it has none of are stand-ins in meego/qml/silica.
  * Own file picker: the N9 indexes pictures and music, not documents.
  * The WLAN name comes from the kernel rather than from connman.
  * poppler, cairo and libjpeg are the device's own, loaded at run time.
  * No office formats: Harmattan has no calligraconverter.

 -- Sebastian Matkovich <sebastian.matkovich@gmail.com>  Mon, 22 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0000
